Q1. What is a Variable in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

A variable is a named memory location used to store data temporarily
during the execution of a PL/SQL program.

Variables are used to store input values, query results, intermediate
calculations and other temporary data required during execution.

Key Points

• Stores data temporarily.
• Declared in the DECLARE section.
• Has a name and data type.
• Can be assigned and modified during execution.

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need Variables in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

Variables are used to store and manipulate data during program
execution. They make PL/SQL programs dynamic and reusable.

Uses

• Store user input.
• Store values fetched from database.
• Perform calculations.
• Pass values between statements.
• Improve code readability.

======================================================================

Q3. What is Variable Declaration?

Interview Ready Answer

Variable declaration is the process of defining a variable by
specifying its name and data type.

At this stage, memory is allocated for the variable.

Example

v_salary NUMBER;

======================================================================

Q4. What is Variable Initialization?

Interview Ready Answer

Variable initialization is the process of assigning an initial value
to a declared variable before it is used.

Example

v_salary NUMBER := 50000;

Important

Declaration

↓

Initialization

======================================================================

Q5. Difference between Variable Declaration and Initialization.

Interview Ready Answer

Variable Declaration

• Defines variable.
• Specifies data type.
• Memory is allocated.
• Value may or may not be assigned.

Variable Initialization

• Assigns value to variable.
• Done after declaration or during declaration.
• Variable becomes ready for use.

======================================================================

Q6. What is %TYPE?

Interview Ready Answer

%TYPE is a PL/SQL attribute that allows a variable to inherit the
data type of a table column or another variable.

Purpose

• Avoid hardcoding data types.
• Prevent data type mismatch.
• Improve maintainability.
• Automatically reflects changes in database schema.

Example

v_salary employees.salary%TYPE;

Important

If the data type of employees.salary changes, the variable
v_salary automatically adopts the new data type.

======================================================================

Q7. Why do we use %TYPE instead of manually specifying the data type?

Interview Ready Answer

%TYPE is used to avoid manually writing data types.

Advantages

• Automatic data type inheritance.
• Less maintenance.
• Prevents data type mismatch.
• Code automatically adapts if column data type changes.
• Better code readability.

======================================================================

Q8. What is %ROWTYPE?

Interview Ready Answer

%ROWTYPE is a PL/SQL attribute that allows a record variable to
inherit the structure of an entire table row or cursor row.

Instead of storing a single value, it stores all columns of a row.

Example

emp_rec employees%ROWTYPE;

Suppose Employees table contains

• employee_id
• name
• salary
• department_id

Then emp_rec contains all four fields.

======================================================================

Q9. Difference between %TYPE and %ROWTYPE.

Interview Ready Answer

%TYPE

• Inherits one column's data type.
• Stores one value.
• Used for normal variables.

Example

salary employees.salary%TYPE;

------------------------------------------------------------

%ROWTYPE

• Inherits entire row structure.
• Stores multiple values.
• Used for record variables.

Example

emp employees%ROWTYPE;

======================================================================

Q10. Difference between SQL and PL/SQL Variables.

Interview Ready Answer

SQL

• Standard SQL does not support procedural variables.
• Uses table aliases and column aliases (not variables).
• Used only for database operations.

PL/SQL

• Supports variables.
• Stores temporary data.
• Used for calculations and business logic.
• Variables exist during program execution.

